menu "espix kernel"

    config ESPIX_KLOG_LINES
        int "Kernel log ring size (lines)"
        range 8 512
        default 256
        help
            Number of lines retained by the kernel log ring that backs `dmesg`.

            A line costs ESPIX_KLOG_LINE_MAX + ~16 bytes: 96 lines is about
            12 kB and 256 about 34 kB. It is PSRAM that pays -- klog.c asks for
            MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM with an internal fallback, and the measurement
            agrees: raising this from 96 to 256 left internal usage unchanged
            (247 kB used either way), so the extra 21 kB came from the pool with
            13 MB in it rather than the one audio, lwIP and the USB host share.

            A ring that wraps loses exactly the lines a fault is read for: at
            96, dmesg reported "507 earlier lines dropped" during one hunt.

    config ESPIX_KLOG_QUIET
        bool "Keep kernel log lines out of the console (dmesg only)"
        default n
        help
            By default espix_klog() output goes to stdout as well as to the ring,
            the way a Linux kernel prints to the console as well as to kmsg.
            Enable this to make the kernel log visible only through `dmesg`.

            Lines captured from ESP_LOGx are never echoed either way — they are
            already heading for the console on their own.

            Phrased as an opt-out rather than an opt-in on purpose: an undefined
            bool is indistinguishable from 'n', so a default-y option silently
            disables itself if the sdkconfig ever fails to regenerate. This way
            the failure mode is the behaviour we want.

    config ESPIX_KLOG_CAPTURE_ESP_LOG
        bool "Capture ESP-IDF log output into the kernel log"
        default y
        help
            Install an esp_log_set_vprintf() hook so ESP_LOGx output from
            ESP-IDF and its components also lands in `dmesg`. Output still goes
            to the console as normal.

    config ESPIX_PSRAM_MB
        int "PSRAM size in MB (0 if none)"
        default 0
        help
            The module's PSRAM size, which ESP-IDF cannot know: on the S3 the
            size is auto-detected at runtime, so R2 and R8 modules share one
            sdkconfig. espix uses it for exactly one thing -- the board identity
            it is built for (model and PSRAM size, e.g. `s3-r8`), which names the
            release assets and the manifest entry the device reads. Flash size is
            deliberately not part of it. Nothing branches on it.

endmenu
